Microservices Architecture with Eureka – GS-Level

Context:
In banking microservices (Payments, Loan, Fraud Detection, Notifications, ATM services), multiple services must communicate reliably and scale independently. Eureka provides service registration and discovery to make this possible.

1️⃣ Architecture Overview
Components

Eureka Server (Service Registry)

Central registry where all microservices register themselves.

Maintains a dynamic list of available service instances.

Health checks remove failed instances automatically.

Eureka Clients (Service Providers & Consumers)

Service Providers register themselves with Eureka.

Example: Payment Service registers /transfer endpoint.

Service Consumers query Eureka to find instances of a required service.

Example: Notification Service queries Payment Service to fetch transaction events.

Load Balancing & Fault Tolerance

Eureka enables clients to pick among multiple instances.

Works with Ribbon, Spring Cloud LoadBalancer, or other load balancers.

2️⃣ Communication Flow in Banking Example

Service Registration

Payment Service starts → registers itself with Eureka Server.

Loan Service starts → registers itself with Eureka Server.

Service Discovery

Notification Service needs to send alerts → queries Eureka for active Payment Service instances.

Load Balancing & Fault Handling

Multiple Payment Service instances available → request routed via round-robin.

If an instance fails → Eureka removes it → clients automatically route to healthy instances.

