4️⃣ Benefits of Eureka in Banking Microservices
Benefit	Banking Impact
Dynamic Service Discovery	No hard-coded endpoints; Payment Service finds Fraud or Notification services automatically
Fault Tolerance	Failed services automatically removed; prevents cascading failures
Load Balancing	Requests distributed across multiple instances during peak hours (e.g., salary day)
Faster Scaling	Auto-register new instances → scale Payment or ATM services independently
Reduced Operational Overhead	Easier deployments; no manual endpoint updates
GS-Level Summary Statement

"In a banking microservices architecture, Eureka acts as a dynamic service registry that enables Payment,
Loan, Fraud Detection, Notification, and ATM services to register, discover, and communicate with each other reliably.
It supports load balancing, fault tolerance, and independent scaling,
ensuring high availability during peak transaction loads and simplifying operations."
3️⃣ Step 1 – Create Eureka Server

Create a Spring Boot project.

Dependencies:

Spring Web
Eureka Server

Main class:

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableEurekaServer
public class DiscoveryServerApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(DiscoveryServerApplication.class, args);
    }
}
4️⃣ application.yml (Eureka Server)
server:
  port: 8761

eureka:
  client:
    register-with-eureka: false
    fetch-registry: false

Run application.

Open:

http://localhost:8761

You will see Eureka Dashboard.

5️⃣ Step 2 – Register Microservice to Eureka

Example: Account Service

Dependency:

Spring Cloud Eureka Client

Main class:

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableDiscoveryClient
public class AccountServiceApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(AccountServiceApplication.class, args);
    }
}
6️⃣ application.yml (Client Service)
spring:
  application:
    name: ACCOUNT-SERVICE

server:
  port: 8081

eureka:
  client:
    service-url:
      defaultZone: http://localhost:8761/eureka

Now run the service.

Dashboard:

ACCOUNT-SERVICE → UP
7️⃣ Multiple Instances (Load Balancing)

Run another instance:

ACCOUNT-SERVICE : 8081
ACCOUNT-SERVICE : 8082

Eureka will show:

ACCOUNT-SERVICE (2 instances)
8️⃣ Calling Service using Service Name

Example:

TRANSACTION-SERVICE → ACCOUNT-SERVICE

Using Spring Cloud OpenFeign:

@FeignClient(name = "ACCOUNT-SERVICE")
public interface AccountClient {

    @GetMapping("/accounts/{id}")
    Account getAccount(@PathVariable Long id);
}

Now request goes through Eureka registry.

